Maintainer risk: HIGH — three independent P0 wire-protocol/correctness defects (taskListHasTask misspelling, per-user routing broken on JSON-pointer patch keys, Task/copy missing same-account rejection and source-id semantics). All three are user-visible: a client following the spec verbatim will get wrong responses today. The integration test suite passes only because every test's oracle is hand-coded against the implementation's actual output, not the spec — workspace test-integrity rule violated. The two P1 maintainer-burden items (isDraft enforcement contributor trap, query_objects sort/filter silent-ignore) compound the risk: the 'reference impl' framing of MemoryBackend teaches consumers the wrong invariants for both isDraft and queryability.
Confidence: 30% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: JMAP-h47t.4 (isDraft contributor trap) — judgement call on whether reference-impl shape matters for production-correctness teaching; JMAP-h47t.11 (six-way duplication) — opinion-flavoured even though it has a clean concrete refactor. Uncertainty driven by: (a) did not exhaustively diff every handler in this crate against the canonical jmap-mail-server sibling (the canonical-template propagation rule means hidden mail-vs-tasks shape divergences would be findings); (b) did not run cargo test --workspace nor cargo clippy --workspace to verify behaviour around isDraft and copy beyond what tests assert; (c) did not inspect every line of memory.rs's get_changes coalescing for state-coalescing edge cases (e.g. update-then-destroy of same id at the boundary state); (d) integration coverage for queryChanges is thin — tests/tasks_tests.rs has no queryChanges test at all, so spec deviations there would not surface. Would raise confidence if: (1) a separate sweep verified every handler matches the canonical mail-server shape line-for-line modulo spec content; (2) probe tests were written for queryChanges add/remove with mutable-property filters; (3) the workspace-wide test-integrity audit (find every test whose expected literal matches an implementation literal rather than a spec literal) was run.
API contract risk (supplement pass): MEDIUM-HIGH — eleven api-contract findings filed (.12 through .22). The two highest-stakes are .22 (TasksBackend rustdocs do not state the workspace-canonical permission-enforcement contract — a consumer reading only docs.rs can ship a backend with no authorisation checks anywhere) and .12 (task_list_has_tasks returns bare bool with no error path, forcing implementors to choose between silent data loss and spurious taskListHasTasks errors on transient backend failure). Three findings (.14, .15, .16) describe contract surfaces where the DEFAULT behaviour is incorrect-for-production but framed in rustdoc as merely a perf knob or a 'single-user' fallback, so a consumer who skims the docs ends up violating spec invariants silently. Two findings (.13, .16) call out divergence from the canonical sibling jmap-mail-server / jmap-calendars-server shape — these should be resolved as workspace-wide propagation sweeps under the canonical-template rule. Two opinion-grade docs findings (.20, .21) round out the pass. Net assessment: the crate's correctness surface is competently implemented (the base reviewer flagged three P0 wire-protocol bugs already), but the trait CONTRACT surface as documented in rustdoc is not yet adequate for a third-party consumer to depend on in production. The contract is largely correct in workspace AGENTS.md — the gap is that AGENTS.md is not what docs.rs visitors read.
API contract confidence: 35% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: .17 (public handle_* fns Value contract) — borderline between 'underspecified' and 'opinion'; whether removing them from the prelude vs documenting them is the right call depends on whether any third-party consumer is known to use them directly (could not verify without searching downstream code). .19 (MemoryBackend Clone semantics) — documented in module rustdoc, so already partially mitigated; whether 'manual Clone impl with own docstring' clears the contract bar or whether it's mostly noise is judgement-call. .21 (line-number citations) — pure docs hygiene, low engineering content. Uncertainty driven by: (a) did not exhaustively inspect every public type's field visibility — AddedItem, ChangesResult, QueryResult, QueryChangesResult are re-exported from jmap-server and their internal field visibility was not checked here; if any have pub fields, the .18-style finding propagates; (b) did not inspect the upstream jmap-server crate's public surface to confirm what flows through the re-exports without docstrings; (c) did not verify whether the README's How-to-use example actually compiles against the current trait signatures (the README shows 'impl TasksBackend for MyBackend' without an explicit 'type CallerCtx = ()' which JmapBackend supertrait requires — possible README rot, but already flagged separately under JMAP-h47t.9 'README diverges from workspace six-section convention and shows wrong trait signature'); (d) the PLAN.md was not read, so the architectural commitments documented there were not cross-checked against the trait shape; (e) no fuzzing was performed on the wire-shape compatibility of handle_* args. Would raise confidence if: (1) a sweep ran 'pub fn' / 'pub struct.*\{.*pub ' grep across the whole crate to find every pub-field; (2) the README compile-ability was verified by hand; (3) the PLAN.md was read for any 'we deliberately chose X over Y' design notes that would re-interpret current findings as intentional.
Idiom rating: ACCEPTABLE — competent Rust, no unsafe (`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` enforced), no error-handling shortcuts in production code (`.unwrap()` confined to Mutex::lock and serialize-of-plain-data sites that are workspace-canonical). Six idiom findings filed (.23-.28): 2 P1 (is_some_and propagation from canonical sibling, catchall Err(_) discards Display content of future variants), 4 P2 (HashMap-clone-to-escape-borrow in recompute_task_lists_with_tasks, unwrap_or(&vec![]) temporary, let-else opportunities, .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>() idiom). Of these the catchall-Err pattern (.24) and the recompute clone (.25) are workspace-wide — same shapes in jmap-mail-server / jmap-chat-server / jmap-calendars-server etc. — so the right resolution is canonical-first propagation rather than a tasks-server-only fix. No idiomatic-rust,unsafe, idiomatic-rust,traits, or idiomatic-rust,macros findings: the trait shape uses `impl Future + Send` returns correctly, no unsafe, the internal `reg!` macro is cookie-cut from canonical jmap-mail-server.
Rust idiom confidence: 25% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: JMAP-h47t.27 (let-else opportunities) — clippy::single_match_else and clippy::needless_return are tasteful suggestions, not bugs; the existing match form is also valid Rust and arguably preserves the "// unreachable: validated above" comment placement better than let-else would. JMAP-h47t.25 (recompute clone) — the canonical-template propagation rule says workspace-wide propagation should be fixed in canonical (calendars-server per JMAP-hwdv.5) first; filing on tasks-server only is arguably misaddressed unless treated as a sweep starter. Uncertainty driven by: (a) did not exhaustively diff every clippy::pedantic-grade lint against the canonical sibling; (b) did not read the `tests/` integration directory — only inline `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` blocks in src/ files were inspected; (c) did not verify whether `as` casts in `memory.rs:524-535` (`position as usize`, `limit.map_or(usize::MAX, |n| n as usize)`) are lossy on 32-bit targets vs the workspace's tested target set — fell back to 'workspace-wide pattern' rather than filing; (d) did not run cargo clippy --workspace -- -W clippy::pedantic to see if the language compiler emits warnings that would surface additional idiom drift; (e) the trait method `is_per_user_property(prop: &str) -> bool` is a static method on a trait — borderline whether that's idiomatic-Rust-bad (a free fn would be more honest about `&self` not being used) but the base reviewer already filed this from a workspace-symmetry angle (JMAP-h47t.16) so I did not double-file.
